terminal-system — AI-assisted two-pane terminal
================================================

Usage: terminal-system [claude|codex]

Opens a tmux session with two panes:

  - Left pane: a normal shell. This is where commands actually run.
  - Right pane: type instructions in natural language, e.g.
    "create user Pippo". The chosen AI engine translates the
    instruction into shell command(s), which are typed into the left
    pane in real time.

Right-pane commands:
  :cmd <command>          type a shell command manually, without AI
  :manual                 prompt for a manual shell command
  :auto on|off            toggle automatic execution (default: off)
  :engine claude|codex    switch AI engine for the rest of the session
  :quit                   end the session

Engine selection:
  Start with "terminal-system codex" or "terminal-system claude" to
  choose an engine explicitly. If the active engine is installed but
  fails at runtime, for example because its quota is exhausted,
  terminal-system tries the other available engine and switches to it
  when the fallback call succeeds.

Confirmation:
  By default every generated or manual command waits for you to press
  Enter (or type "e" to edit it, or "n" to cancel it) before it runs. With
  ":auto on", safe commands run automatically — but a fixed set of
  dangerous patterns (rm -rf /, mkfs, dd onto /dev, fork bombs,
  chmod -R 777 /, shutdown/reboot, killall -9, userdel/groupdel on
  root/sudo/admin, iptables -F, writes onto common block devices under
  /dev) always stops for manual confirmation regardless of auto-mode.
  When a command is likely to ask for input, such as sudo, ssh, passwd,
  an editor, or a pager, focus moves to the left pane so passwords and
  other interactive prompts are typed into the shell running the
  command, not into the instruction pane. When the command finishes and
  the left pane returns to the shell, focus moves back to the
  instruction pane. Non-interactive commands keep focus on the
  instruction pane so you can keep asking questions.

Session memory:
  Instructions, generated commands, and recent left-pane output are
  kept as context for the rest of the session (so "now add him to the
  sudo group" can refer back to a user you just created), but nothing
  is written to disk — history disappears when the session ends.

Requires:
  The claude and/or codex CLI already installed and authenticated.
  Neither is a hard package dependency.
